Two thread races in the U6 path, both silent, both fatal above 2 threads.

Symptom: equilibrium_gap 8.7e-08 with one assembly thread, 0.3..1.4 and
non-reproducible run to run with four or more.  E_eff swung between
5.1e9 and 1.98e10 on one deck.  This was briefly misread as inf-sup
instability -- it is not; the element is sound.

RACE 1 -- the !$omp directive that never existed.

  u6patch.f guarded its one-time node->element map build with
  !$omp critical(u6map).  But FFLAGS is "-Wall -O2 -cpp": no -fopenmp at
  COMPILE time (it appears only on the link line), and ccx parallelises
  with pthreads, not OpenMP.  Every !$omp line in a .f file is therefore
  an inert comment, and all assembly threads allocated and filled
  nstart/nlist simultaneously.

  Fixed with u6lock.c, a pthread mutex (u6lock_/u6unlock_) -- the right
  primitive for ccx's actual threading model, and it carries the memory
  barrier that publishes the finished map to the other threads.

RACE 2 -- a widened local array is not where a narrow one was.

  Patch 0006 widened the user-element matrix to su/smu(150,150).
  gfortran places a local array over ~32 kB in STATIC storage rather than
  on the stack, so a 180 kB su became one array shared by every pthread:

      readelf -sW mafillsm.o
        su.1   0x2bf20 OBJECT LOCAL .bss
        smu.0  0x2bf20 OBJECT LOCAL .bss

  The stock s(60,60) is 28.8 kB and stays on the stack, which is exactly
  why ccx has never tripped over this.  Fixed by making su/smu
  allocatable: heap per invocation, released automatically on return.
  Verify with `size -A mafillsm.o | grep bss` -- it must be 0.

After both fixes E_eff is bit-identical for 1, 2, 4, 8 and 16 threads,
and equilibrium_gap is 8.663e-08.
